# InteliBoy's sshd configuration.
#
# This replaces the stock sshd_config the openssh package installs. It has to
# be a whole file rather than a fragment: the stock one ships every option
# commented out, so the compiled-in defaults apply, and the default for
# PermitRootLogin is 'prohibit-password' - root cannot log in with a password
# at all. There is no Include directive in it either, so a drop-in would do
# nothing. The distro overlay is applied after the packages, so this wins.
#
# What this is for: this is the development distro, and ssh is the affordance
# that makes it debuggable at all - it has no getty on tty1 and a renderer that
# owns the screen. Access is by key, from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys, so the
# image contains no secret: a copy of it grants nobody anything.
#
# Measured on the hardware before choosing this. 'passwd -l root' - which is
# what build-distro.sh leaves behind when no password is configured - denies
# *public key* logins too, not just passwords: this sshd is built without PAM
# and treats a '!'-prefixed shadow field as a locked account rather than an
# unusable password. An appliance built that way is unreachable. A '*' field
# denies every password and leaves key authentication working, which is what
# this distro asks for.

# Root logs in by key and by nothing else. 'prohibit-password' is the precise
# form: root may authenticate, but not with anything typed.
PermitRootLogin prohibit-password
PubkeyAuthentication yes

# Both of these are already the compiled-in defaults. They are written down
# because relying on an unstated default is exactly what went wrong the first
# time: the stock file leaves PermitRootLogin commented out, its default is
# 'prohibit-password', and root could not log in at all until this file said
# otherwise. A default that is not written down is a default that changes.
PasswordAuthentication no
PermitEmptyPasswords no

# There is deliberately no 'UsePAM' here. OpenSSH is built without linux-pam,
# and a build without it rejects the option outright - 'Unsupported option
# UsePAM' on every start - rather than accepting it as a no-op. sshd
# authenticates against /etc/shadow either way.

# The host keys are generated on first boot by the boot script, not shipped:
# a key baked into the package would be the same on every device built from
# it, with the private half sitting in the build tree.
HostKey /etc/ssh/ssh_host_rsa_key
HostKey /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ecdsa_key
HostKey /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key

Subsystem sftp /usr/libexec/sftp-server
